    "result_text": "All tests pass \u2014 **29 run, 0 failures, 0 errors**, including the new `Cp18Tests` (city-capacity rule).\n\n## What I changed\n\nThe `POST /api/owners` pipeline now rejects a create when the owner's city already contains 50+ owners, responding **409 Conflict**. Following the project's OfficeFloor function-injection conventions:\n\n1. **`RejectCityAtCapacity.java`** (new function step) \u2014 reads the validated request via `@Val OwnerFieldsDto`, counts existing owners in the same city (case-insensitive, matching `AssignCustomerCode`'s convention), and throws when the count is \u2265 50.\n\n2. **`CityAtCapacityException.java`** (new checked escalation) \u2014 carries the city name.\n\n3. **`CityAtCapacityExceptionHandler.java`** + its escalation YAML \u2014 respond 409 with an RFC-7807 `ProblemDetail`, mirroring the existing `DuplicateTelephoneException` handler.\n\n4. **`owners.POST.yml`** \u2014 inserted the `rejectCityAtCapacity` step (governed by `transaction`) after the duplicate-household check and before `build`, so the request is rejected before any entity is constructed or saved.\n\nThe threshold is \"reject when the city already contains 50 or more\" \u2014 so the 51st owner in a city fails, which is exactly what `Cp18Tests` asserts (fill a fresh city to 50, then expect the next create to be 409).",
